+# Veterinarian Directory Is Filterable by Specialty, Not Searchable by Free Text
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declared search out of scope for every entity but the owner: only owners are searchable, and the veterinarian directory was a read-only paged listing. The rationale was that owner search already demonstrates paged prefix search once, and repeating it elsewhere would add surface without teaching anything new.
+
+A request now asks the directory to be narrowable to the veterinarians holding one specialty — an attribute the page already displays. That request sits against NG-9's boundary, so the boundary has to be read before the capability can be built: is narrowing by a displayed attribute the same declined thing as free-text search, or a distinct capability?
+
+The owner decided (2026-08-24) that it is distinct.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Leave NG-9 whole and decline the request.** Rejected: it conflates two different interactions. Free-text search matches typed text against a name with a prefix rule; filtering matches a whole, already-shown attribute value. Declining the second because the first was declined blocks a capability on a boundary that was never about it.
+2. **Open veterinarian search entirely.** Rejected: free-text veterinarian search would repeat the owner-search pattern the sample already shows once, which is exactly what NG-9's rationale rules out.
+3. **Narrow NG-9 to admit attribute filtering while keeping free-text search out** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+NG-9 is narrowed. Filtering the veterinarian directory by a specialty it already displays is in scope, on both the HTML page and the machine-readable route, as a whole-value case-insensitive match. Free-text search for a veterinarian — or for a pet or a visit — stays out of scope. Owners remain the only entity searchable by free text.
+
+The reinstated machine-readable route is a supported surface under a fresh requirement, `REQ-VET-003`; the previously withdrawn `REQ-VET-002` stays withdrawn and its ID is not reused. The specialty filter is `REQ-VET-004`, the first requested capability on that surface.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-9 leaves the open-provenance consultation for this boundary: the line between free-text search (out) and attribute filtering (in) is now a recorded owner decision, not a survey artifact.
+- The veterinarian directory gains one filter capability. Narrowing NG-9 further, or adding free-text veterinarian search, remains a separate recorded owner decision.
+- A visible filter control on the page is deliberately not part of this decision; the filter is a URL contract only, and a control may follow.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed NG-9 row.
+- [PRD Veterinarian directory](../prd.md#req-vet-004) — `REQ-VET-003` (supported machine-readable surface) and `REQ-VET-004` (specialty filter).
