+# Veterinarian Specialty Filter Is Applied In the Controller Over the Cached Collection
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`REQ-VET-004` narrows both veterinarian surfaces — the paged `/vets.html` page and the machine-readable `/vets` route — to the veterinarians holding one named specialty. The match is on the whole specialty name and ignores letter case; a blank or spaces-only value is treated as absent, and an unmatched specialty yields an ordinary empty result.
+
+The veterinarian directory reads a small, seeded collection through `VetRepository`, whose `findAll()` and `findAll(pageable)` are both `@Cacheable("vets")` with no write path and no eviction. The whole-collection variant already backs the `/vets` route; the paged variant backs the page. The question is where the filter lives: a new Spring Data query on `VetRepository`, or a predicate in `VetController` over the cached collection.
+
+Owner search matches at the database (`findByLastNameStartingWith`), and that choice carries a recorded consequence: case-insensitivity is reached three different ways across H2, MySQL, and PostgreSQL, and the PostgreSQL schema fails it (a Known Defect against `REQ-OWN-002`). A specialty filter pushed to the database would inherit that same vendor-divergent case-folding surface.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Add a Spring Data query** such as `findBySpecialtiesNameIgnoreCase(String, Pageable)`. Rejected: `IgnoreCase` folds case per the vendor's collation, reintroducing the owner-search vendor divergence for a brand-new capability; a `WHERE` over the eager `@ManyToMany` join reopens the collection-fetch-with-pagination pitfall; and the new query key does not share the existing cached collection.
+2. **Filter in `VetController` over the cached `findAll()` collection** (chosen). One `equalsIgnoreCase` on the specialty name is uniform across all three vendors, reuses the single cached collection both surfaces already read, and matches the whole name trivially. The page paginates the filtered list in memory — safe because the collection is small, seeded, and cached.
+
+## Decision
+
+The filter is applied in `VetController`. Both handlers normalize the specialty (a null, blank, or spaces-only value, after trimming surrounding spaces, means no filter — the same handling the empty owner search gives) and, when a filter is present, retain the veterinarians holding a specialty whose name equals it case-insensitively. The page paginates the filtered list in memory and carries the specialty on every paging link; the machine-readable route wraps the filtered list in the existing `Vets` element. `VetRepository`, `Vets`, `Vet`, and `Specialty` are unchanged.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Case-insensitive matching is uniform across H2, MySQL, and PostgreSQL, because the fold happens in Java rather than in the schema. The filter does not inherit the owner-search vendor divergence.
+- The cached collection is reused, so the filter adds no database round trip and no `@ManyToMany` pagination hazard. In-memory paging is acceptable only while the directory stays a small seeded collection; a directory that grew large would force this decision back to a database query.
+- The filter predicate lives in the controller, consistent with the feature-package-without-a-service-layer shape where query shaping (owner-search normalization) already sits in the controller.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003, REQ-VET-004
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md#contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the `VetController` row realizing this decision.
+- [Veterinarian Directory Is Filterable by Specialty, Not Searchable by Free Text](2026-08-24-non-goal-veterinarian-search.md) — the product boundary this decision implements.
